{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Those who curse you are cursed, and those who bless you are blessed.</b> But concerning Balaam it says the reverse of this (Numbers 24:9), and so it was said to Abraham (Genesis 12:3): ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ אָאֹר״. It appears that there are two kinds of cursers and two kinds of blessers. One kind is the one who literally curses or literally blesses, and the rule for the righteous is always thus: their beginning is suffering and their end is tranquility. Now, every one who blesses presumably blesses at a time when a blessing is needed, that is, at a time when suffering is prevalent — to exclude the time of tranquility, when they do not need a blessing, for so the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Balaam when he said ״אִם כֵּן אֲבָרְכֵם״, He said to him, 'They do not need your blessing, for he is already blessed.' And every one who curses presumably curses at a time of peace, for at a time of suffering why should he add a curse? And of this kind it was said to Abraham first: ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ״, for at the beginning there will be suffering and then you will need those who bless, and all who bless you — I will bless. And afterward, at the time of peace, many will be jealous of you and will scheme to curse you, and I will curse them. And of this kind Balaam said: ״מְבָרְכֶיךָ בָרוּךְ וְאֹרְרֶיךָ אָרוּר״." "But the 'those who curse you' that he mentions here does not speak at all of cursers in the language of a curse, but rather of those who cause distress, and so Rashi explained. And this is found most prevalently at a time of suffering, after the manner of (Shabbat 32a) 'the ox fell and needed a knife' — for in addition to the suffering, they will come to distress you in the very place where they ought to have comforted you, for at a time of peace, with what would they cause distress? And likewise 'those who bless you' means 'those who gladden you,' and this is found most prevalently at a time of tranquility, when many will rejoice with you. From here Rashi learned to interpret אֹרְרֵיהֶם as meaning מְצַעֲרֵיהֶם ('those who distress them'), and by this all the doubts and difficulties that the Ibn Ezra and the Ramban raised are resolved — see in their books. But according to Rashi's own interpretation all this does not fit, and I have come only to resolve the verses. You will know that this is so, for behold, Balaam said (Numbers 23:10) ״וּתְהִי אַחֲרִיתִי כָּמֹהוּ״, and how then could he say (Numbers 24:9) ״אֹרְרֶיךָ אָרוּר״ at the end, and foretell for them that their end would be suffering? Rather, it is certain that 'those who curse you' speaks of the time of tranquility that will be at the end of their days, and concerning that he said ״וּתְהִי אַחֲרִיתִי כָּמֹהוּ״. But for the wicked He foretold the reverse of this and said (Numbers 24:20) ״רֵאשִׁית גּוֹיִם עֲמָלֵק וְאַחֲרִיתוֹ עֲדֵי אֹבֵד״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, foretold also to Abraham that his beginning would be in distress and he would need blessers, and his end would grow exceedingly great, and cursers would come upon him and would not prevail against him."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:35.418556106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}